<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Thurii</h1><p class="st-meta">444 BCE – 282 BCE (approx.) · capital Thurii</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Panhellenic colony founded under Athenian sponsorship near ruined Sybaris in 444 BCE, linked with Herodotus and Protagoras.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After several attempts by Sybarite survivors to refound their city, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> organised a new colony open to settlers from all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Thurii was founded in 444 or 443 BCE near the old site, with a street plan attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodamus-of-miletus">Hippodamus</a> and laws attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protagoras">Protagoras</a>. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> is said to have joined it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The settlers soon quarrelled; the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybarites</a> claimed privileges and were expelled, and the citizen body was organised in ten tribes by origin. Thurii prospered from the Sybarite plain and fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/siris">Siris</a>, a dispute settled by the joint foundation of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> in 433 BCE. Lucanian pressure grew, and in 389 BCE they crushed a Thurian army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laus">Laus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Threatened again by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a>, Thurii appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which placed a garrison there in 282 BCE; the move provoked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> and led to the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a>. Thurii later suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Hannibalic War</a>, and in 193 BCE Rome founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony of Copia on the site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/thurii.jpg" alt="Thurii" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Peter Stewart · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sybaris_archeological_park_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>444 BCE – 282 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thurii</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thourioi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-363&amp;focus=thurii" aria-label="Thurii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5086 755 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Thurii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5406 1011L5407 1012M5406 1011L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5407 1012h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5406 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5406 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-363&amp;focus=thurii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
